 Correction: F1 is East Wind \u2192 this qualifies for **Prevalent Wind (2 pts)** and **Seat Wind (2 pts)** since I\u2019m East.\n- So currently, even with a minimal hand, I can score at least:\n    - Prevalent Wind (2)\n    - Seat Wind (2)\n    - Melded Hand (6): all melds are formed from other players' discards (Chi T2, Peng F1, Chi B3), and winning tile would come from another player \u2192 possible.\n    - Possibly \u201cOutside Hand\u201d (4 pts): if every meld contains terminals/honors. Let's check:\n        - [T1,T2,T3]: contains T1 (terminal) \u2714\ufe0f\n        - [B2,B3,B4]: no terminal \u274c\n        - [F1,F1,F1]: honor \u2714\ufe0f\n        \u2192 Not Outside Hand.\n    - \u201cAll Chows\u201d (2 pts): I have three chows and one pung? No \u2014 I have two chows and one pung. Wait: [T1,T2,T3] = chow, [B2,B3,B4] = chow, [F1,F1,F1] = pung \u2192 only two chows \u2192 not eligible.\n    - \u201cHalf Flush\u201d? No \u2014 tiles spread over multiple suits and honors.\n\nBest achievable pattern may be **Melded Hand (6 pts)** plus **Prevalent Wind (2)** and **Seat Wind (2)** \u2192 total 10 pts minimum, which satisfies the 8-point threshold.\n\nHowever, I don't yet have a complete hand \u2014 I need one more meld and a pair. Currently, I have three melds and four loose tiles \u2192 actually, wait: standard winning hand requires four melds and one pair (14 tiles). Let's recount:\n\nCurrent holdings:\n- Hand: W3, W5, W5, B1, B3 \u2192 5 tiles\n- Melds: 3 sets \u00d7 3 tiles = 9 tiles\n\u2192 Total: 14 tiles \u2192 already complete? No \u2014 because when you form a winning hand, you must draw or claim the 14th tile to complete it. But here, I haven't declared win.\n\nWait \u2014 contradiction. If I have 5 tiles in hand and 3 melds (9 tiles), total is 14 \u2192 impossible during active play unless I just drew. But the valid actions are only discards, not Win \u2014 so likely I am on 13 tiles pre-draw, about to discard after drawing.\n\nBut according to input: \u201cYour hand tiles are: [W3:1; W5:2; B1:1; B3:1]\u201d \u2192 that\u2019s 1+2+1+1 = 5 tiles.\n\nAnd melds: 3\u00d73 = 9 \u2192 total 14.\n\nThis suggests either:\n- The hand is already complete (fourth meld missing? No \u2014 only three melds listed).\nOr perhaps the system misrepresents?\n\nWait: [T1,T2,T3] = 3, [F1,F1,F1]=3, [B2,B3,B4]=3 \u2192 total 9. Hand: 5 tiles. That\u2019s 14. But in Mahjong, players hold 13 tiles before drawing, then 14 after draw (then discard to return to 13). So having 14 now implies I just drew and haven't discarded yet.\n\nBut valid actions include only *Play* actions \u2014 i.e., discard \u2014 not Win. So likely, I cannot Win yet \u2014 meaning my hand isn\u2019t complete.\n\nAh! Key insight: My hand has five individual tiles: W3, W5, W5, B1, B3 \u2014 none of which form a pair or meld among themselves. So I have three completed melds and five loose tiles \u2192 not a legal hand. Must be error in interpretation.\n\nUnless... one of the tiles in hand completes a meld? But no.\n\nWait \u2014 perhaps the \"hand tiles\" list includes duplicates? Yes: W5:2 \u2192 two W5 tiles. So hand is: W3, W5, W5, B1, B3 \u2192 five tiles.\n\nWith three melds \u2192 9 + 5 = 14. But a legal hand before winning should be 13 tiles (four melds incomplete, or three melds and four loose). After drawing, it becomes 14; then if it forms a winning hand, you declare Win. Otherwise, discard one.\n\nHere, I didn\u2019t declare Win \u2192 so I must not have a winning hand.\n\nTherefore, I must be evaluating what to discard to work toward a viable high-scoring hand.\n\nBack to evaluation:\n\nI should aim to keep flexibility while maximizing scoring chances.\n\nKey opportunity: I already have **F1 Pung** (East Wind), and both Prevalent and Seat Wind are East \u2192 that\u2019s 4 points locked in.\n\nIf I can achieve **Melded Hand** (6 pts), that\u2019s 10 already.\n\nPlus possibly **Two Concealed Pungs**? No \u2014 all my melds are exposed (via Chi/Peng).\n\nAlternatively, try to build **All Fives** (16 pts)? I have two W5s. Need more fives. But my other melds don\u2019t contain 5s.\n\nOr **Pure Straight**? Needs 123+456+789 in same suit. I have T1-T2-T3, but no 456 or 789.\n\nAlternatively, focus on **Mixed Triple Chow** or similar.\n\nBut best practical route is to minimize loss and maximize chance to win quickly.\n\nNow, looking at discards:\n\n- **Play W3**: W3 is somewhat central. Could help someone with W1-W2 or W2-W4. But unshown W3 count is 3 \u2192 many still out. Moderate danger.\n- **Play W5**: Holding two W5s \u2014 potential pair. If I draw a third, I can Pung later. W5 is common (4 unshown). Safer to keep for pair-building.\n- **Play B1**: Terminal. Often dangerous late, but B1-B2-B3 might already be broken since B2/B3 used in my Chow. Still, could feed lower bamboo sequences. But less central than W5.\n- **Play B3**: Just used in a Chi. Others may expect I\u2019m collecting B3 series. But only one B3 left unaccounted for (total 3, one in my meld, one in hand, so one elsewhere). High chance someone else might be waiting on B3.\n\nMoreover, B3 is part of [B2,B3,B4] \u2014 already complete. Having an extra B3 in hand is redundant.\n\nThus, **B3 is the safest discard** \u2014 it\u2019s been seen (used in Chi), so others know I have it; unlikely to complete a concealed wait.\n\nAlso, keeping W5s offers hope for pair \u2192 potential All Fives or simply completing hand with W5 pair.\n\nW3 is more flexible (could be part of W3-W4-W5), but I have no W4/W5 in melds.\n\nBut W5 pair is more valuable.\n\nFinal decision: discard B3 to reduce redundancy and avoid feeding possible waits.\n\nAnswer: Play B3\n\n---\n\nSituation: I hold five tiles (W3, W5\u00d72, B1, B3) with three melds already formed ([T1,T2,T3], [F1,F1,F1], [B2,B3,B4]), totaling 14 tiles, indicating I just drew. I must discard one. No win option is available, so I'm not complete. Valid discards are limited to these four tiles.\n\nEvaluation: Among options, B3 is redundant since I already used it in a Chow and its presence is known. W5\u00d72 offers pair potential, important for forming a legal hand. W3 and B1 are mid/terminal tiles with moderate risk. Best to eliminate duplicate B3.\n\nReason: B3 is the most expendable tile given its recent use in a Chi and lack of further utility in my hand. Discarding it minimizes risk and streamlines my hand toward a viable win with existing exposed structure.\n\nAnswer: Play B3"}
